\title{bst260projhelp: table extraction and bibliographic integrity for PDF reports}
\description{
Two families of tools, bundled together because they recur on the same
kind of source document (a long government/NGO report distributed only
as PDF):
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Table extraction} (\verb{pdf_*}): recovering structured tables from PDF
pages, either by simple line-based parsing when a table doesn't wrap
(\code{pdf_read_count_pct_table()}), or by clustering word coordinates when
it does (\code{pdf_read_table()}, \code{pdf_read_wide_wrapped_table()}).
\item \strong{Bibliographic integrity} (\verb{rand_*}, \verb{vancouver_*}): parsing a
report's own reference list, resolving its in-text citations against
that list, and exporting the result as BibTeX or CSL-JSON so it can
be checked, reused or linked. Two citation styles are covered,
matching the two worked examples: \verb{rand_*} for RAND-style per-chapter,
author-year reference lists, \verb{vancouver_*} for numbered
Vancouver-style reference lists.
}

See \code{vignette("gun-legislation", package = "bst260projhelp")} and
